;`\n\n**Scans the query forces**\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database evaluates `lower()` on every row.\n2. **Full scan of `orders`, or a hash join over all of it.** Unless `orders.user_id` is indexed, each matching user forces a scan of `orders`. Many databases, such as Postgres, don't create indexes on foreign keys automatically.\n3. **Wide row reads from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables, including duplicated column names like `id`. That rules out index-only scans and can break clients that map columns by name.\n\n**Fixes**\n\nIndex the expression, so the lookup on `users` becomes an index seek:\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n```\nIndex the join key so the `orders` side is a seek too:\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n```\nName the columns you need:\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = lower('a@example.org');\n```\nI wrote `o.id`, `o.created_at` and `o.total` as placeholders. Swap in the columns you actually use.\n\n**Alternative to the expression index:** if you can store emails already lowercased, or use a case-insensitive type or collation (`citext` in Postgres, or a `_ci` collation in MySQL), you can drop `lower()`. Then a plain index on `email` works, and a `UNIQUE` constraint also prevents case-variant duplicates.\n\n**Other points**\n- Hard-coding the email is fine for a one-off. In application code, use a bound parameter instead (`= lower($1)`) to avoid SQL injection and to allow plan reuse.\n- Put `users` first in the `FROM` clause. The planner will reorder joins anyway, but this shows the intent.\n- Check the result with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE)` after adding the indexes. On small tables the planner may still pick a seq scan, which is correct.\n\nI haven't changed `query.sql`. I can apply the rewrite if you want. I'd need the real column list, and I'd need to know which database you're on, since the syntax above is Postgres-flavored.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}